Speed of Light Analysis Notes:

Hardware Setup
I used an AMD Ryzen 7 8840HS laptop CPU with 8 cores, 3.301 GHz clock, AVX2 support, and dual channel DDR5 5600 RAM.
The peak FP32 compute is 422.5 GFLOPs per second and peak memory bandwidth is 89.6 GB per second. 
The roofline crossover point is 4.7 FLOPs per byte.

Model Workload and Calculation Error
I hand calculated the FLOPs and bytes for the heavy layers. During Conv2, I initially forgot to add the bias parameters, calculating 2,426,880 bytes. 
I corrected this by adding the 32 output channel biases to get 2,427,008 bytes. 
The arithmetic intensity barely changed, proving the layer is compute bound, but I verified the corrected math using the thop library.

SOL Predictions
Using the max formula for compute and memory limits, I predicted a forward pass for batch size 1 takes 0.92 ms. 
For batch size 32, it takes 29.5 ms. A full training step takes 73.8 ms, using a 2.5x multiplier to account for the backward pass and optimizer updates. 
One full epoch on the BRISC dataset takes 9.81 seconds, calculated using 133 steps for 4,250 images.

Actual Measurements
I measured the real world performance using a Python script with time.perf_counter, taking the median of 100 runs after 10 warmup runs. 
The forward pass for batch size 1 took 21.22 ms, batch size 32 took 560.09 ms, and a full training step took 1118.73 ms. 
The actual backward pass overhead was about 2.0x, which was close to my 2.5x prediction.

Efficiency Results
The forward pass achieved 4.35 percent of the theoretical speed of light, and the training step achieved 6.60 percent.
This gap is expected for a small model on a CPU due to Python overhead and memory management.

